 Stage 3 of `docs/Designs/Active/Apps/ClockAndZone.md`: the analogue face.

 WHAT MAKES A CLOCK LOOK WRONG IS A HAND THAT MISSES ITS TICK, so the geometry
 is asked before anything is drawn. The table has sixty positions because a
 clock has sixty, and every one is a whole number of degrees; if that is true
 then a hand and the tick it points at are computed from the same number and
 cannot disagree. The rows below say it is true by asking the circle for its
 cardinal points and its identity, which no table of plausible-looking numbers
 satisfies by accident.

Section: Body

  cf-yn : Boolean -> Text
  cf-yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "NO"

 THE CARDINALS ARE EXACT AND THE REST IS NOT ASKED TO BE. Twelve, three, six
 and nine o'clock are the four positions a reader can check without a
 calculator, and a table that is right at those and wrong between them is a
 table that was transcribed rather than computed.

  cf-cardinals : Text
  cf-cardinals =
    "cardinals: t0 sin " & show (face-sin 0) & " cos " & show (face-cos 0)
    & "   t15 sin " & show (face-sin 15) & " cos " & show (face-cos 15)
    & "   t30 sin " & show (face-sin 30) & " cos " & show (face-cos 30)
    & "   t45 sin " & show (face-sin 45) & " cos " & show (face-cos 45)
    & "  exact " & cf-yn (face-sin 0 == 0 & face-cos 0 == 1000 & face-sin 15 == 1000 & face-cos 15 == 0 & face-sin 30 == 0 & face-cos 30 == 0 - 1000 & face-sin 45 == 0 - 1000 & face-cos 45 == 0)

 SIN SQUARED PLUS COS SQUARED IS THE WHOLE TABLE AT ONCE. It is asked at every
 one of the sixty positions rather than at a few, because a single transposed
 digit shows here and nowhere else: the shape stays a closed loop and the
 hands still sweep, but one of them is short. The tolerance is two parts in a
 thousand, which is what rounding sixteen sines to three digits costs.

  cf-ident : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cf-ident (t) (n) (worst) =
    if t >= n then worst
    else
      let v = face-sin t * face-sin t + face-cos t * face-cos t
      in let e = if v > 1000000 then v - 1000000 else 1000000 - v
      in cf-ident (t + 1) n (if e > worst then e else worst)

  cf-identity : Text
  cf-identity =
    let w = cf-ident 0 60 0
    in "identity: worst error over 60 positions " & show w
       & "  within two parts per thousand " & cf-yn (w <= 2000)

 THE SCREEN'S Y GROWS DOWNWARD AND A CLOCK'S TWELVE IS UP, which is the one
 place this arithmetic is not the arithmetic of a unit circle. A face whose
 vertical component was not negated draws a clock that runs backwards and
 looks entirely normal until somebody reads it.

  cf-updown : Text
  cf-updown =
    "up: dy at twelve " & show (face-dy 0 100) & "  at six " & show (face-dy 30 100)
    & "  dx at three " & show (face-dx 15 100) & "  at nine " & show (face-dx 45 100)
    & "  twelve is up " & cf-yn (face-dy 0 100 < 0)
    & "  three is right " & cf-yn (face-dx 15 100 > 0)

 THE HOUR HAND MOVES WITH THE MINUTES, which is the difference between a clock
 and a picture of one. At half past ten it sits between ten and eleven, and a
 hand that jumped on the hour reads as broken to anyone who has looked at a
 real one. The row asks the whole hour, the half hour and the next hour, so a
 hand that moved too far is refused as well as one that did not move.

  cf-hour : Text
  cf-hour =
    "hour hand: 10:00 " & show (face-hour-t 10 0) & "  10:30 " & show (face-hour-t 10 30)
    & "  11:00 " & show (face-hour-t 11 0) & "  12:00 " & show (face-hour-t 12 0)
    & "  advances " & cf-yn (face-hour-t 10 30 > face-hour-t 10 0)
    & "  and stays short of the next hour " & cf-yn (face-hour-t 10 30 < face-hour-t 11 0)
    & "  twelve wraps to the top " & cf-yn (face-hour-t 12 0 == 0)

Section: Pixels

  cf-w : Integer = 64
  cf-h : Integer = 64
  cf-cells : Integer = 4096
  cf-bg : Integer = 0
  cf-rim : Integer = 3355443
  cf-tick : Integer = 8947848
  cf-hand : Integer = 15790320
  cf-acc : Integer = 16733525

  cf-fill : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cf-fill (b) (i) (n) (c) =
    if i >= n then 0 else let p = poke-32 b (i * 4) c in cf-fill b (i + 1) n c

  cf-at : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cf-at (b) (x) (y) = peek-32 b ((y * cf-w + x) * 4)

  cf-render : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cf-render (h) (mi) (s) =
    let b = alloc-bytes (cf-cells * 4)
    in let z = cf-fill b 0 cf-cells cf-bg
    in let d = face-draw b cf-w cf-h (layout-rect 0 0 cf-w cf-h) 32 32 30 h mi s cf-rim cf-tick cf-hand cf-acc
    in b

 A PIXEL THAT IS NEITHER THE BACKGROUND NOR ANY OF THE FOUR COLOURS IS A
 BLEND, and blends along the dial's edge are the antialiasing. The count is
 the measurement: a hard-edged circle has none, and the staircase it draws
 instead is the same defect the rounded corners had.

  cf-blend : Integer -> Boolean
  cf-blend (v) = v /= cf-bg & v /= cf-rim & v /= cf-tick & v /= cf-hand & v /= cf-acc

  cf-count-row : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cf-count-row (b) (y) (x) (acc) =
    if x >= cf-w then acc
    else cf-count-row b y (x + 1) (acc + (if cf-blend (cf-at b x y) then 1 else 0))

  cf-count : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cf-count (b) (y) (acc) =
    if y >= cf-h then acc else cf-count b (y + 1) (acc + cf-count-row b y 0 0)

 The centre must be painted and the far corner must not: a face that filled
 its whole box would score well on every other row here while covering the
 pane it sits in.

  cf-pixels : Text
  cf-pixels =
    let b = cf-render 10 10 30
    in "pixels: blended " & show (cf-count b 0 0)
       & "  the rim is soft " & cf-yn (cf-count b 0 0 > 0)
       & "  centre painted " & cf-yn (cf-at b 32 32 /= cf-bg)
       & "  corner clear " & cf-yn (cf-at b 1 1 == cf-bg)
       & "  outside the radius clear " & cf-yn (cf-at b 32 0 == cf-bg)

 THE SECOND HAND IS FOUND WHERE IT WAS SENT. At thirty seconds it points
 straight down, so the accent colour must appear below the centre and must not
 appear above it. This is the row that would catch a sign error the cardinals
 above cannot: those ask the table, this asks the drawing.

  cf-has : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  cf-has (b) (x) (y0) (y1) (col) =
    if y0 >= y1 then False
    else if cf-at b x y0 == col then True
    else cf-has b x (y0 + 1) y1 col

  cf-second : Text
  cf-second =
    let b = cf-render 10 10 30
    in "second hand at 30: below the centre " & cf-yn (cf-has b 32 40 56 cf-acc)
       & "  above it " & cf-yn (cf-has b 32 8 24 cf-acc)
       & "  which is down " & cf-yn (cf-has b 32 40 56 cf-acc & cf-has b 32 8 24 cf-acc == False)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    act
      print-line-uni cf-cardinals
      print-line-uni cf-identity
      print-line-uni cf-updown
      print-line-uni cf-hour
      print-line-uni cf-pixels
      print-line-uni cf-second
      print-line-uni "clock-face: reached the end"
    end
